WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.700
Prove that any whole number multiplied by the next whole

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:05.410
number gives an even result.

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:07.390
Three times four is twelve.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.810
Six times seven is forty-two.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:13.520
Checking examples is a good start, but the proof must

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:15.190
cover numbers we have not checked.

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:23.300
Whole numbers alternate: even, odd, even, odd.

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:27.200
So of any two neighbouring numbers, exactly one is even.

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:30.560
There are two cases: the first number is even,

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:32.260
or the first number is odd.

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:35.250
If the first number is even, it is two

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:36.180
lots of something.

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:39.490
Multiply it by the next number and the result is still

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:40.680
two lots of something.

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:42.380
So the product is even.

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:45.250
If the first number is odd, then the next

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:46.160
number is even.

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:49.370
It is two lots of something, so again the product is two

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:50.220
lots of something.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:51.720
The product is even.

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:53.960
A picture confirms it.

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:56.500
Arrange the product as a rectangle of dots.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.540
It splits into two identical staircases.

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:02.760
Two identical halves means the total is even,

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:05.370
and each half is one plus two plus three,

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:06.720
up to the first number.

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:11.070
The two cases cover every whole number, and each gives

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:12.080
an even product.

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.770
Rebuild the proof, then explain why the product of three

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:18.920
whole numbers in a row is always a multiple of three.
